// case: list-parameter-has-no-element-type
// what: the element type is decided where a list is BUILT -- a literal, or the
//      first push into an empty one -- and a `list` PARAMETER is not built
//      anywhere the checker can see. So a helper declared over a list is
//      declared over nothing in particular, and a read inside it is untyped:
//      the destination register is `void*` and the accessor is the untyped one.
//
//      That is F-0009, and it is the half of F-0002 that a list element type
//      does not reach. The direct forms are all fixed -- `n.at(1)` over
//      `[10,20,30]` is 20, a list built by push reads back typed, a `for` over
//      one does not segfault -- and this still crashes, because the int comes
//      back out of a `-> string` function and `print` dereferences it:
//
//        $ orbit run tests/negative/n39_list_parameter_has_no_element_type.orb
//        Segmentation fault (core dumped)
//
//      Nothing is pinned on the value: a crash moves per run and per machine,
//      and a pin that fires at random teaches people to ignore the pin. The
//      case asserts its class, which is the part that can be checked -- the
//      front end accepts a program that cannot work.
//
//      FIXING IT needs `list<string>` in a signature, which is generics (F-0007,
//      queued as slot 7) and not a change to the runtime. Until then the honest
//      answer is the header on std/collections/lists.orb, which already says the
//      string type is not enforced and why.
// known-defect: F-0009
fn getOr(l: list, i: int, dflt: string) -> string {
    if i < 0 { return dflt }
    if i >= l.len() { return dflt }
    return l.get(i)
}

fn main() -> int {
    var nums = []
    nums.push(10)
    nums.push(20)
    print(getOr(nums, 0, "d"))
    return 0
}
